ggml-zendnn: make the MoE expert gates measurable, and they hold

GGML_ZENDNN_MAX_EXPERTS and GGML_ZENDNN_MIN_ROWS_PER_EXPERT expose the two
MUL_MAT_ID thresholds, defaults unchanged at 32 and 32, and both refusals are now
counted rather than returned silently. Also GGML_ZENDNN_ALLOW_MUL_MAT_ID, which
lifts the blanket decline.

Asked to check max_experts=128 so a 128-expert MoE model "runs properly". It
already ran properly -- correctness never depended on these gates, only on which
backend gets the ops. Measured on qwen3-coder-30b-a3b:

  gates at default                  tg 3.4
  max_experts=128 only              no change in dispatch: 7998 rejections
                                    appear because the NEXT gate refuses them
  max_experts=128 + min_rows=0      tg 0.7, 0.8   -- experts dispatch, 4-5x slower

Raising max_experts alone does nothing: the ops clear the expert-count gate and
are refused by N/n_experts <= 32, since MoE decode routes about eight active rows
across 128 experts. Lifting both makes them dispatch -- accepted rises 3696 to
4216 -- and decode collapses to a quarter.

Structural, not a tuning miss: the grouped path is gather + per-expert GEMM +
scatter, and with 128 experts and eight active rows each expert gets at most one
row, so every call is a GEMV wrapped in overhead that does not shrink with the
work.

Both defaults stay. Capturing the MoE opportunity -- still real, the iGPU margin
there is 1.23x against 2.2x dense -- needs each ACTIVE expert dispatched as its own
decode GEMV with no gather or scatter, which is a change to the backend's
MUL_MAT_ID path rather than a threshold.

Note for whoever revisits: the executed counter lives in the MUL_MAT compute path
and MUL_MAT_ID does not touch it, so expert execution is invisible in the stats
table. That hid the effect at first.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-zendnn/ggml-zendnn.cpp	2026-08-17 13:47:23.756733067 +0000
+++ ggml/src/ggml-zendnn/ggml-zendnn.cpp	2026-08-17 14:48:51.646320218 +0000
@@ -948,7 +948,22 @@
                     // qwen3-coder-30b-a3b killed llama-bench with signal 6 that
                     // way. Accepting an op the library cannot serve is worse
                     // than declining it, so decline.
-                    if (op->op == GGML_OP_MUL_MAT_ID) {
+                    // GGML_ZENDNN_ALLOW_MUL_MAT_ID=1 lifts this to find out
+                    // whether it is still true. The decline was added when a
+                    // MUL_MAT_ID reached AOCL-DLP and aborted, BEFORE
+                    // group_matmul gained a per-expert matmul_direct route for
+                    // GGML per-group INT8 on non-VNNI hosts. The backend already
+                    // slices the weight per expert -- batch_wei is
+                    // src0->data + cur_a*nb02 -- so the shape reaching the
+                    // library is 2-D and may now be expressible.
+                    static const bool allow_mmid = [] {
+                        const char *e = std::getenv("GGML_ZENDNN_ALLOW_MUL_MAT_ID");
+                        return e != nullptr && std::atoi(e) != 0;
+                    }();
+                    if (op->op == GGML_OP_MUL_MAT_ID && !allow_mmid) {
+                        if (zendnn_dispatch_stats::enabled()) {
+                            g_zendnn_stats.rejected[weights->type]++;
+                        }
                         return false;
                     }
                     // Only the quantised types this fork actually has kernels
@@ -996,14 +1011,36 @@
                 // MUL_MAT_ID's gather+matmul+scatter approach favors a moderate expert count
                 if (op->op == GGML_OP_MUL_MAT_ID) {
                     const int64_t n_experts = weights->ne[2];
-                    const int64_t max_experts = 32;
+                    // GGML_ZENDNN_MAX_EXPERTS and GGML_ZENDNN_MIN_ROWS_PER_EXPERT
+                    // make both of these measurable instead of arguable. The
+                    // defaults are unchanged; qwen3-coder-30b-a3b has 128 experts
+                    // and so is refused by the first, and MoE decode routes about
+                    // eight active rows across all of them, so it is refused by
+                    // the second too -- which is why lifting only the earlier
+                    // blanket MUL_MAT_ID decline changed nothing at all.
+                    static const int64_t max_experts = [] {
+                        const char *e = std::getenv("GGML_ZENDNN_MAX_EXPERTS");
+                        const long v = (e != nullptr) ? std::atol(e) : 0;
+                        return (v > 0) ? (int64_t) v : (int64_t) 32;
+                    }();
+                    static const int64_t min_rows_per_expert = [] {
+                        const char *e = std::getenv("GGML_ZENDNN_MIN_ROWS_PER_EXPERT");
+                        const long v = (e != nullptr) ? std::atol(e) : -1;
+                        return (v >= 0) ? (int64_t) v : (int64_t) 32;
+                    }();
                     if (n_experts > max_experts) {
+                        if (zendnn_dispatch_stats::enabled()) {
+                            g_zendnn_stats.rejected[weights->type]++;
+                        }
                         return false;
                     }
 
                     // fall back once the average rows per expert (N / n_experts) is too thin
                     // to amortize each per-expert GEMM's overhead
-                    if (N / n_experts <= 32) {
+                    if (N / n_experts <= min_rows_per_expert) {
+                        if (zendnn_dispatch_stats::enabled()) {
+                            g_zendnn_stats.rejected[weights->type]++;
+                        }
                         return false;
                     }
                 }
